  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates downstream benchmark performance across model sizes, a different measure and task from pretraining loss, and reports how performance varies with scale.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports downstream CAKE benchmark scores, not held-out pretraining loss or fitted scaling laws. The model scores bear on V6; the separate +think and +tool comparisons are not evidence about power-law loss scaling.",
    "test_reason": "A new domain benchmark (cloud-native architecture) scored across released models of 0.5B–70B within three families. Its results bear on V6 only, and only weakly: it is a cross-sectional comparison of released models, with no loss measure, no controlled scaling and no fitted predictive relation. It suits T3 (new setting) better than T2 (different method, same question).",
    "authors_conclusion": "Broadly correct. The authors conclude that MCQ accuracy nears its ceiling above 3B, while free-response scores scale steadily and keep differentiating models across sizes. They add that the two formats capture different facets of knowledge, that training data composition matters beyond parameter count, and that +think helps free-response while +tool hurts models below about 8B.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "A downstream cloud-architecture knowledge benchmark, not a pretraining-loss scaling-law measurement; no power-law fitting, extrapolation, compute sweep, or allocation optimization.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Observed performance depends on evaluation format and model family; the paper reports a slight Qwen FR dip from 3B to 7B and a Llama FR plateau from 3B to 8B. +think and +tool variants also alter scores, but do not test pretraining-loss scaling.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Answer conviction (unanimity across three shuffled runs) rises with size: Qwen 0.5B 56% vs Qwen 7B 96% at recall level.",
        "proposed_claim": "Answer stability across reruns increases with model size.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "Tool augmentation degrades small models along a size gradient (Llama 1B −23.9 pp, 3B −53.0 pp, 8B −2.3 pp MCQ).",
        "proposed_claim": "A minimum model size is needed for augmentation to help.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/timadam03/CAKE-benchmark",
    "peer_reviewed": "2026 IEEE 23rd International Conference on Software Architecture Companion (ICSA-C)",
    "problems": "The benchmark covers cloud-native architecture only. Free-response scores use a single primary judge (DeepSeek-R1:32B), with validation by Gemini 2.5 Pro noted. The paper reports no uncertainty intervals for model scores, and the MCQ metric approaches a ceiling at larger sizes. The results are downstream benchmark performance, not pretraining loss.",
    "rows_left_out": "No tested-version rows were omitted. The paper also reports scores for +think and +tool configurations and per-topic analyses; these were not extracted because they are augmentation or topic-specific comparisons rather than base-model size results bearing directly on the tested scale trend.",
    "untested": [
